  by any script on the origin, and survives logout. I'll design "remember me" to store the username only, and I'll
  write that constraint into the spec explicitly.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What shape should the stored preferences take? → Fixed set + defaults (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Two design details I'll decide myself rather than spend a question on, unless you object when you see the design: if
  [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is unavailable (Safari private mode, disabled cookies, quota exceeded) the module falls back to an
  in-memory store so the app still runs; and a corrupt/unparseable stored value falls back to the declared default
  rather than throwing.

  Last question before I present the design. This repo has [1mno configured tooling[0m — no test runner, no linter, no [38;5;153mscripts
[39m  block. Since preferences storage is exactly the kind of module that wants tests (defaults, corrupt data, missing
  backend), it's worth deciding now; setting it up is cheapest before the code exists.

  - [1mUnit tests only[0m — a runner plus a first passing test. Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m needs no dependencies, but testing
    browser code means the module must be importable outside the browser, which is a real design constraint (it pushes
    [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m behind an injectable backend — which I was going to do anyway for the in-memory fallback).
  - [1mLint + format too[0m — ESLint + Prettier. Buys consistency, costs a [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m in a repo that currently has zero
    dependencies.
  - [1mNothing[0m — keep the repo dependency-free and untested. Fastest, and leaves the persistence edge cases unverified.

  [1mMy recommendation: unit tests with [38;5;153mnode:test[39m, no linter.[0m It keeps the repo dependency-free (Node ships the runner),
  and it covers the failure modes that actually matter here. A linter on four files is overhead without much payoff.
